canonical decoder Stage 0 + Stage 1 read-only audit (2026-08-15)

Decision: REVISE the singular "canonical decoder" into one canonical-encoding protocol
with three typed semantic decoders. Do not build one generic semantic decoder.

Scope
- D-1 wager.w5_independence_family.
- D-2 reference_models.arbitrarily_large_ax_core_discrete.
- D-3 reference_models.arbitrarily_large_three_layer, which composes D-2.
- Future order-9 promotions are explicitly out of scope.
- No implementation, schema, manifest, fixture, tolerance, gate, commit, or push change
  was made by this audit.

Q1. Common mechanism and required granularity

Common protocol
All three inputs can share only a small structural protocol:
1. a schema version;
2. the existing contract ID and exact Lean declaration name;
3. explicit theorem parameters, currently k/k0;
4. duplicate-free complete finite carrier descriptions;
5. closed constructor tags chosen from a contract-specific finite enum;
6. literal payload tables where the Lean definition contains finite data;
7. rejection of callbacks, opaque predicates, supplied proof Booleans, unknown fields,
   missing fields, and noncanonical aliases.

This common layer performs parsing, normalization, completeness checks, and dispatch. It
does not define the semantic meaning of a tag and must not generate expected semantic
values for its own tests.

D-1 typed bridge: W5CanonicalEncoding
- Lean evidence: formal/ERIEC/Wager/Richness.lean:9-26 defines richImpl k0 and its W5
  proof; lines 28-35 define poorImpl and its not-W5 proof; lines 39-41 bind the pair.
- Required identity granularity: complete Fin k0 and Unit state/action encodings, fixed
  RawCore tag, dc values, hinge tables, and the step/other FrozenImpl field tags even
  though W5 observes only dc and hinge. Checking only hinge cardinalities identifies an
  arbitrary witness, not the fixed richImpl/poorImpl objects.
- Semantic decoder: wager-specific; it maps rich_impl_v1 and poor_impl_v1 tags to the
  line-mapped FrozenImpl definitions and independently evaluates W5 on complete states.

D-2 typed bridge: AXCoreCanonicalEncoding
- Lean evidence: formal/ERIEC/RefModel/LargeCore.lean:103-128 defines the nested stable,
  dynamic, and AX-core witness fields; lines 149-184 construct the canonical witness.
- Required identity granularity: k, complete Fin k/Bool/RefState carriers; all DC data;
  DynFrame phi/theta/drift/kappa/epsilon/omega/stepInt constructor tags; TotalNext;
  dc_eq/frame_eq; cardinalities; multivalued and hinge fields; R2Prime; E5;
  dynamic.stable identity; same_stable.
- Semantic decoder: reference-model-specific. It must map tags separately to
  largeAXCoreDC, largeDynFrame, largeTotalNext, largeRefDrift, largeRefExternal,
  largeRefCore, and largeRefCoreIso. An opaque function sampled on a few inputs is not a
  canonical encoding.

D-3 typed bridge: ThreeLayerCanonicalEncoding
- Lean evidence: formal/ERIEC/RefModel/LargeCore.lean:138-143 defines tier1, bridge,
  world_nontrivial, value_one, and section13_2; lines 190-202 construct the witness.
- Required identity granularity: a complete D-2 encoding; dependent identity between
  bridge.dc and tier1.stable.dc; stableWorld and its exact nonzero fixed direction;
  normalized value data; and every NondegenerateReferenceWitness field from
  formal/ERIEC/RefModel/Nondegenerate.lean:48-54.
- Semantic decoder: a three-layer composition decoder. It may call the D-2 decoder and
  the existing independent section-13.2 validator, but its bridge/world/value composition
  rules are not shared with W5 or the D-2 leaf decoder.

Conclusion for Q1
The three needs share a canonical-encoding protocol, not one semantic decoder. Use three
names and three typed schemas/decoders. Sharing carrier-normalization helpers is safe;
sharing the tag-to-Lean semantic mapping is not. D-3 may compose D-2 explicitly.

Q2. Meaning of "checked"

Claude's reading is substantially correct: the main change is an input-schema change
from opaque functions and caller-supplied truth summaries to closed structural data.
That converts extensional function equality into recognition of a versioned constructor
description. It is schema plus structural correspondence checking, not a new proof of the
already-proved Lean theorem.

"Checked" must mean all of the following, not merely "the decoder returned successfully":
- The parser rejects every value outside the closed typed schema.
- The schema contains no executable callback or proof-result Bool.
- Every constructor tag is bound in the existing manifest row to one Lean declaration
  and to a line-by-line mapping in the basis log.
- Parameter and carrier checks are performed before decoding.
- Literal finite payloads are compared structurally and exhaustively.
- The decoded finite semantics are evaluated by the contract checker; acceptance is not
  just tag recognition.
- Independent tests use hand-written literal expected projections and mutations. They do
  not call the decoder to manufacture expected values.
- Every tag, parameter, carrier, nested identity, and semantic field has a rejection case.
- A schema version changes whenever a mapped Lean definition or payload layout changes.

This is a checked correspondence boundary, not a machine-checked Lean theorem that the
Julia decoder is extensionally equal to Lean. The residual trusted base must be stated in
the manifest assumptions; these contracts must not be promoted beyond the relation that
this evidence supports.

Q3. Avoiding a new self-confirmation surface

Required safeguards
1. Typed separation: implement three semantic decoders. Do not create a generic
   tag->object semantic registry used by all three contracts.
2. Independent oracle data: freeze literal positive descriptors and literal expected
   field projections in tests or a reviewed fixture. The production decoder must not
   generate either side of its own comparison.
3. Mutation completeness: reject unknown tags, wrong versions/declarations/parameters,
   incomplete or duplicate carriers, every wrong literal table, every broken nested
   identity, and semantic counterexamples. Include at least one valid-k conclusion
   mutation, not only a failed premise.
4. Source binding: each manifest guarantee/basis log maps decoder tags to exact Lean
   definitions and lines. A source change invalidates review until the mapping is audited.
5. Decode/validate separation: parsing and tag dispatch produce a normalized typed value;
   the contract checker separately evaluates the relevant finite semantic conditions.
6. No opaque escape hatch: reject callbacks, custom comparator functions, precomputed
   expected values, and supplied proof flags.
7. Composition isolation: D-3 may consume the public result of D-2, but W5 shares no
   semantic mapping with the reference-model decoders. A D-2 failure must be visible as a
   named D-3 tier1 failure rather than silently normalized away.

Manifest visibility
For the order-11 minimum, expose each decoder's schema version, tags, trusted-base
assumption, and line mapping in its existing D-1/D-2/D-3 manifest row and basis log. This
makes the correspondence visible without pretending it is a separate Lean theorem.

Do not add a new "decoder contract" in the minimum implementation. A certified decoder
contract would require a new Lean declaration specifying the encoding semantics, catalog
registration, contract-count changes, G1-G4, and a user-approved contract target. Adding
only a Julia manifest row would violate the catalog/manifest ID equality. Such a formal
decoder contract belongs to the out-of-scope reusable/full version.

Residual limitation
No ordinary Julia test suite can prove the decoder correct for all future code changes.
The safeguards remove circular expected-value generation and make the trust boundary
auditable; they do not eliminate it. If Stage 2 proposes one shared semantic decoder or
decoder-generated expected fixtures, Q3 is unresolved and implementation must stop.

Q4. Minimum order-11 version versus reusable full version

Minimum version that can complete order-11
- Add private/versioned typed descriptors or strictly validated NamedTuple schemas local
  to src/wager.jl and src/reference_models.jl. Do not export a new generic decoder API.
- Implement W5CanonicalEncoding, AXCoreCanonicalEncoding, and
  ThreeLayerCanonicalEncoding independently; D-3 composes D-2.
- Add literal positive and field-mutation negatives to test/test_wager.jl and
  test/test_reference_models.jl.
- Update only the existing three manifest rows and their basis log, in addition to the
  other order-11 rows already identified.
- Rough scale: several hundred Julia/test lines, dominated by AX-core field coverage. It
  is not a small wrapper, but it avoids new Lean declarations, catalog IDs, and exports.

Reusable/full version
- A generic canonical-encoding AST, public decoder registry/API, formal Lean syntax and
  semantics for descriptors, decoder correctness statements, new certified contracts,
  catalog/ledger integration, and possible future order-9 migrations.
- This is materially larger, changes public API and contract/catalog counts, and requires
  a separate user-approved batch. It is not needed to finish the current order-11 scope.

Q5. Coupled-invariant effects

Minimum typed-decoder design
- Contract count remains 157; no new contract ID or catalog entry.
- Exact set remains 29 because D-1 becomes sound_only and D-2/D-3 become
  witness_validator; sensitivity registry and sensitivity_registry_sha256 remain unchanged.
- pilot_relations lines 73-79 are affected only by the separate order-11 change of
  worlddc.no_unconditional_equivalence to sound_only. The decoder targets themselves are
  not listed in pilot_relations.
- CertifiedArtifact.lean, specs/ledger.toml, and specs/claim-ledger-v2.toml remain unchanged.
- Existing D-1/D-2/D-3 manifest rows change relation/assumptions/guarantee/basis_log only.
- If Stage 2 exports new descriptor types or changes existing method behavior instead of
  adding overloads, that is a public-API change and must return to the user.

Reusable/full design
- New formal decoder contracts would change CertifiedArtifact, manifest count, likely
  v1/v2 ledger bindings, G1-G4 evidence, and possibly public exports. Exact registry/hash
  changes only if a new or migrated row actually claims exact_finite_decision.
- These effects are why the full design is a separate decision, not hidden in order-11.

Q6. Decoder creation does not guarantee D-2/D-3 promotion

D-2 must not be promoted if any of these remains true:
- the input contains an opaque function, predicate, comparator, or supplied proof Bool;
- dc_eq/frame_eq is inferred from samples rather than a closed canonical descriptor;
- R2Prime is tested on selected subsets rather than established by the recognized
  largeRefDrift constructor semantics;
- E5, TotalNext/internal_total, dynamic.stable identity, same_stable, computed hinge, or
  any nested DC/Frame field is omitted;
- a line-mapped tag or independent field mutation test is missing.

D-3 must not be promoted if any of these remains true:
- D-2 does not pass its full validator;
- bridge.dc identity with tier1.stable.dc is not structural and explicit;
- direction_nonzero/direction_fixed or world_nontrivial uses a relaxed/new tolerance;
- value_one is not exact;
- any section13_2 field is omitted or accepted from a supplied summary Bool;
- D-3 swallows or reconstructs a failing D-2 result.

Both remain at their conservative current relation (or the Stage-2-selected
complete_only relation) if the canonical schema cannot satisfy these conditions. Building
decoder code is not itself evidence for promotion.
